Sure, here's an example of how you can use C# to connect to a SQL database and retrieve the data you need:
```
using System;
using System.Data.SqlClient;
class Program
{
    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        string connectionString = "Data Source=myServerAddress;Initial Catalog=myDataBase;User ID=myUsername;Password=myPassword";
        string query = "SELECT p.ProductName, AVG(r.Rating) AS AvgRating, COUNT(s.SaleID) AS SalesCount " +
                       "FROM Products p " +
                       "LEFT JOIN Ratings r ON p.ProductID = r.ProductID " +
                       "LEFT JOIN Sales s ON p.ProductID = s.ProductID " +
                       "WHERE p.Category = 'Shirt' AND p.LastUpdated < DATEADD(day, -30, GETDATE()) " +
                       "GROUP BY p.ProductName " +
                       "HAVING AVG(r.Rating) >= 3 AND COUNT(s.SaleID) > 0 " +
                       "ORDER BY COUNT(s.SaleID) DESC";
        using (SqlConnection connection = new SqlConnection(connectionString))
        {
            SqlCommand command = new SqlCommand(query, connection);
            connection.Open();
            SqlDataReader reader = command.ExecuteReader();
            while (reader.Read())
            {
                string productName = reader.GetString(0);
                double avgRating = reader.GetDouble(1);
                int salesCount = reader.GetInt32(2);
                Console.WriteLine("{0} - Avg Rating: {1:F2} - Sales Count: {2}", productName, avgRating, salesCount);
            }
        }
    }
}
```
Note that you'll need to replace the `connectionString` variable with the appropriate connection string for your SQL database. You'll also need to modify the SQL query to match the names of your tables and columns.
This code uses a `LEFT JOIN` to include all products, even if they don't have any ratings or sales. It then filters the results using the `WHERE` and `HAVING` clauses to only include products in the "Shirt" category that haven't been updated recently, have an average rating of 3 or higher, and have made at least one sale in the last month. Finally, it sorts the results by the number of sales made in the last month.

Here's some Go code that can generate three unique and effective ways for a family to conserve energy in their daily routine using the letters of the word "CONSERVATION":
```
package main
import (
    "fmt"
    "math/rand"
    "time"
)
func main() {
    rand.Seed(time.Now().UnixNano())
    // Define the letters of the word "CONSERVATION"
    letters := []rune("CONSERVATION")
    // Generate three unique techniques
    techniques := make([]string, 0)
    for i := 0; i < 3; i++ {
        // Randomly select three unique letters from the word
        techniqueLetters := make([]rune, 0)
        for len(techniqueLetters) < 3 {
            letter := letters[rand.Intn(len(letters))]
            if !contains(techniqueLetters, letter) {
                techniqueLetters = append(techniqueLetters, letter)
            }
        }
        // Generate a technique using the selected letters
        technique := ""
        for _, letter := range techniqueLetters {
            technique += string(letter)
        }
        techniques = append(techniques, technique)
    }
    // Print the generated techniques
    fmt.Println("Three unique and effective ways to conserve energy:")
    for _, technique := range techniques {
        fmt.Println("- " + technique)
    }
}
// Helper function to check if a slice contains a given element
func contains(slice []rune, element rune) bool {
    for _, e := range slice {
        if e == element {
            return true
        }
    }
    return false
}
```
This code randomly selects three unique letters from the word "CONSERVATION" and generates a technique using those letters. It repeats this process three times to generate three unique techniques. The `contains` function is a helper function that checks if a given slice contains a given element.

