How can I calculate the probability that the incorrect delivery was caused by the address error and not by other factors such as weather or traffic using the binomial distribution formula in PHP? 
Suppose I have recorded the shipping information of 50 packages, including the shipping address, date of shipment, and delivery status. To test the theory that errors in the delivery address might be causing delays, I intentionally changed one digit in the shipping address of 10 packages and tracked their delivery. After tracking these packages, I found that 7 out of 10 packages were delivered to the wrong address. 
Here's the PHP code I have so far:
$n = 10; // number of trials
$p = 0.7; // probability of success
$k = 7; // number of successes
// calculate the binomial coefficient
function binomialCoeff($n, $k)
{
    $res = 1;
    // Since C(n, k) = C(n, n-k)
    if ($k > $n - $k)
        $k = $n - $k;
    // Calculate value of [n*(n-1)*---*(n-k+1)] / [k*(k-1)*---*1]
    for ($i = 0; $i < $k; ++$i)
    {
        $res *= ($n - $i);
        $res /= ($i + 1);
    }
    return $res;
}
// calculate the probability
function binomialProb($n, $p, $k)
{
    $prob = binomialCoeff($n, $k) * pow($p, $k) * pow(1 - $p, $n - $k);
    return $prob;
}
$prob = binomialProb($n, $p, $k);
echo "The probability that the incorrect delivery was caused by the address error is: " . round($prob, 2);

