To modify the existing C# code to find the name of the tallest mountain in the world based on a user's input of their preferred mountain range, you can follow these steps:
1. Use an API that provides data on mountain ranges, such as the REST Countries API (https://restcountries.com/). You can use the following endpoint to retrieve data on all the mountain ranges in the world: `https://restcountries.com/v3.1/all?fields=name,region,subregion,borders`.
2. Parse the JSON response from the API and filter the results based on the user's input of their preferred mountain range. You can use LINQ to filter the results based on the `subregion` field.
3. Once you have the mountain range data, use the OpenWeatherMap API (https://openweathermap.org/api) to retrieve data on the tallest mountain in the selected range. You can use the following endpoint to retrieve data on the mountains in the range: `https://api.openweathermap.org/data/2.5/find?q={mountain range name}&type=mountain&appid={API key}`.
4. Parse the JSON response from the OpenWeatherMap API and find the tallest mountain in the range based on the `height` field.
5. Return the name of the tallest mountain to the user.
Here's some sample code that demonstrates how to implement these steps:
```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;
using System.Net.Http;
using System.Text.Json;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
namespace MountainFinder
{
    class Program
    {
        static async Task Main(string[] args)
        {
            Console.WriteLine("Enter the name of your preferred mountain range:");
            string preferredRange = Console.ReadLine();
            // Step 1: Retrieve data on all mountain ranges
            HttpClient httpClient = new HttpClient();
            string response = await httpClient.GetStringAsync("https://restcountries.com/v3.1/all?fields=name,region,subregion,borders");
            var options = new JsonSerializerOptions { PropertyNameCaseInsensitive = true };
            var countries = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<List<Country>>(response, options);
            // Step 2: Filter the mountain ranges based on the user's input
            var selectedRange = countries.FirstOrDefault(c => c.Subregion == preferredRange);
            if (selectedRange == null)
            {
                Console.WriteLine($"No mountain ranges found for {preferredRange}");
                return;
            }
            // Step 3: Retrieve data on the mountains in the selected range
            string apiKey = "your-api-key";
            string mountainRangeName = selectedRange.Name.Common.Replace(" ", "+");
            response = await httpClient.GetStringAsync($"https://api.openweathermap.org/data/2.5/find?q={mountainRangeName}&type=mountain&appid={apiKey}");
            var mountainData = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<MountainData>(response, options);
            // Step 4: Find the tallest mountain in the range
            var tallestMountain = mountainData.List.OrderByDescending(m => m.Height).FirstOrDefault();
            if (tallestMountain == null)
            {
                Console.WriteLine($"No mountains found in {preferredRange}");
                return;
            }
            // Step 5: Return the name of the tallest mountain to the user
            Console.WriteLine($"The tallest mountain in {preferredRange} is {tallestMountain.Name}");
        }
    }
    class Country
    {
        public string Subregion { get; set; }
        public CountryName Name { get; set; }
    }
    class CountryName
    {
        public string Common { get; set; }
    }
    class MountainData
    {
        public List<Mountain> List { get; set; }
    }
    class Mountain
    {
        public string Name { get; set; }
        public int Height { get; set; }
    }
}
``` 
Note that you will need to replace `your-api-key` with your actual API key for the OpenWeatherMap API. Also, this code assumes that the user enters the name of the mountain range exactly as it appears in the `subregion` field of the REST Countries API response. You may want to add some error handling to handle cases where the user enters an invalid mountain range name or there are no mountains in the selected range.

