To optimize a binary search tree implementation in Scala to handle concurrent write operations, we can use a locking mechanism to ensure thread safety during insertions and deletions. One way to do this is to use a ReentrantReadWriteLock, which allows multiple readers to access the tree concurrently, but only one writer at a time.
In the example implementation below, we add a lock to each node in the tree. When inserting a new value, we acquire a write lock on the node where the new value should be inserted. This ensures that no other thread can modify the tree at the same time. We then recursively insert the new value into the left or right subtree, and release the lock.
Similarly, when searching for a value, we acquire a read lock on each node we visit. This allows multiple threads to search the tree concurrently, as long as they are not modifying it.
Here's an example implementation:
```scala
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantReadWriteLock
sealed trait BST
case object Empty extends BST
case class Node(value: Int, left: BST, right: BST, lock: ReentrantReadWriteLock) extends BST
object BST {
  def insert(tree: BST, value: Int): BST = {
    tree match {
      case Empty => Node(value, Empty, Empty, new ReentrantReadWriteLock())
      case n @ Node(v, l, r, lock) =>
        if (value < v) {
          lock.writeLock().lock()
          val updatedL = insert(l, value)
          Node(v, updatedL, r, lock)
        } else if (value > v) {
          lock.writeLock().lock()
          val updatedR = insert(r, value)
          Node(v, l, updatedR, lock)
        } else {
          n // value already exists in the tree
        }
    }
  }
  def search(tree: BST, value: Int): Boolean = {
    tree match {
      case Empty => false
      case Node(v, l, r, lock) =>
        lock.readLock().lock()
        val result =
          if (value < v) search(l, value)
          else if (value > v) search(r, value)
          else true // value found
        lock.readLock().unlock()
        result
    }
  }
}
```
In this implementation, we acquire a write lock when inserting a new value, and a read lock when searching for a value. This ensures that only one thread can modify the tree at a time, but multiple threads can search it concurrently.

