Here's an example C++ function that takes an integer value (n), adds a random number between 1 and 10 to it, and returns the nth character of a randomly selected joke from the array:
```
#include <iostream>
#include <cstdlib>
#include <ctime>
const char* catJokes[] = {
  "Why don't cats play poker in the jungle? Too many cheetahs.",
  "Why did the cat join Instagram? To stalk meow celebrities.",
  "What do you call a cat who likes to eat beans? A purrito.",
  "Why do cats make terrible storytellers? They only have one tail.",
  "What do you call a cat that loves to bowl? An alley cat.",
  "Why don't cats like online shopping? They prefer a cat-alog."
};
char getNthCharacter(int n) {
  // Get a random joke from the array
  srand(time(NULL));
  int randomIndex = rand() % (sizeof(catJokes) / sizeof(*catJokes));
  const char* joke = catJokes[randomIndex];
  // Add a random number between 1 and 10 to n
  int randomNum = rand() % 10 + 1;
  n += randomNum;
  // Return the nth character of the selected joke (starting at 0)
  if (n < strlen(joke)) {
    return joke[n];
  } else {
    return '\0';
  }
}
int main() {
  // Example usage
  std::cout << getNthCharacter(5) << std::endl; // Output: d
  return 0;
}
```
Note that the `srand()` function is used to seed the random number generator with the current time, so that the random numbers generated are different each time the program is run. The `strlen()` function is used to get the length of the selected joke, so that the function can properly handle cases where n exceeds the length of the selected joke. In these cases, the function returns the null character (`'\0'`).
